+# Transport query diagnostic v1 — SOURCE only
+
+This is a separately versioned diagnostic of the retained minimum incoming
+failure, not another ordinary-client attempt. It is limited to one baseline:
+publish the existing2907-byte public synthetic signed outer, then issue its exact
+112-byte query by ID/limit1. There is no Android/Amethyst app/proxy, NSEC entry,
+new key generation, signing/encrypt/decrypt, Rust native validator, file profile
+or SDK. The malformed unused incoming plan NSEC is not copied or corrected.
+Optional modeled APP REQ bursts are explicitly deferred beyond this v1 baseline.
+
+## Frozen operands and isolation
+
+Event ID `1203eaed1d1c6fd2d3fe9600209c3bc2036e68e9a7b79bb8b720989d06b844da`.
+The outgoing publish Buffer and query Buffer remain exact archived bytes:
+SHA256 `844e64e2790a9833880aedc3016e9da82130a29d9e415858d0d46fdcb8946494`
+and `ae042185e892c9a385b7add6442e478834817d8ed310f8967e7dc3d4bd689389`.
+Replaying identical public signed ciphertext is not encrypting different
+plaintext under a reused key/nonce. No encryption/nonce profile is reinterpreted.
+
+Use the exact existing runtime/rs-relay image digests, Node/ws installed tree,
+unchanged guard/interpreter bundle and mounted relay config. Original three
+cgroup caps remain anchor128MiB/64PID/1CPU, relay512MiB/128PID/1CPU and
+fixture1024MiB/128PID/1CPU; memory=swap. Anchor has network none; the two services
+share only its private namespace. No host ports/device/GUI/shared clipboard or
+SDK/APK mounts. No new infrastructure manager: the copied original finite
+command/ownership/identity/cleanup helpers remain explicit and diffed. The only
+start/validation changes close roles to three, remove Android methods/branches
+and bind the same guard bundle readonly in every role. The borrowed guard ENV
+contains unused historical SDK path strings; no SDK is mounted or invoked and
+no ADB method/guest route exists.
+
+Every runtime command requires current source/config/dependency pins, an exact
+fresh root release, actual SOURCE/control acceptance and fresh admission receipt.
+The release is externally authenticated by the coordinator; JSON labels alone
+cannot prove a real instruction or reviewer. Fresh output/run ID/lease and actual
+original outer tool responses must be preserved. This preparation creates only
+an unexecuted config candidate, never a release/lease or acceptance. No old
+failed window donates a row. Diagnostic success does not change E02 PARTIAL22,
+E04/E06 prerequisites or Q01/Q08/Q13/Q15/Q27 BLOCKER classifications.
+
+## Guard and timeout preservation
+
+The lease guard is byte-identical. It enforces the same closed original lease
+with UTC/MONOTONIC/BOOTTIME, rollback/drift/observation-gap refusal and exact owned
+child lifecycle. Default proposed diagnostic lease300s is shorter; maximum
+remains1800s and includes setup. It is never renewed. Original service ceiling
+1800s, parent fixture command55s and Unix control reply45s remain unchanged.
+Network timeout10000ms starts at the original pre-open position; WebSocket
+handshake remains3000ms. Synchronous guard calls remain in the same exchange
+entry/open/send/send-callback/backend-message positions. Original1000ms watchdog
+also remains. Instrumentation is not an asynchronous guard approximation, timer
+restart, relaxed EOSE/query criterion or measured speed improvement.
+
+Only source extraction/metadata AST/hash operations occurred here. Prepared
+fake-socket models never execute the actual guard; real guard behavior still
+needs actual runtime qualification under a fresh grant. Existing unchanged
+guard29 is an explicit prior byte-bound scope, not new execution. Source/cleanup
+mock definitions are independent of runtime interoperability.
+
+## Phase evidence and refusal boundaries
+
+`exchange.mjs` is an instrumented sibling of the preserved original function.
+Its runtime context is fixed to real pinned ws, process.hrtime.bigint, built-in
+timers, unchanged synchronous Python guard and bounded local evidence writer.
+Model injection is only an internal test interface; no config/CLI can inject a
+different clock, timer, socket or guard. The existing strictJSON and semantic
+whole-object equality functions are extracted unchanged; no crypto library is
+imported to obtain them.
+
+Each phase row has schema `e02-transport-query-phase-v1`, positive sequence,
+ISO UTC string, `monotonicNanoseconds` as a canonical nonnegative decimal string,
+phase name and only its source-emitted fields. Global capture cap16MiB and frame
+cap262144 bytes remain. The monotonic string is process-local timing, never a
+relaxation of existing wire integer rules or a CPU/RSS/PSI measurement.
+
+Phases include guard entry/ok/refused, exchange start/timer armed, socket open,
+send intent/invoke entry/returned/callback entry/guard passed, raw backend callback
+entry/guard passed, socket error/close, timer firing and resolved/rejected outcome.
+A raw callback-entry row is written before guard solely as refusal-context
+evidence. It never authorizes a send, decode, forward, validation or contribution
+after guard failure. Terminal callbacks are context only. The only post-failure
+socket action is cleanup termination of that exact owned socket. First failed
+control closes its own server/sockets; host command failure revokes the original
+shared lease and executes exact-CID cleanup. No retry or later case follows.
+
+The frozen source's old pre-send row was merely intent. Here SEND_INVOKE_RETURNED
+separately records returning from ws.send; SEND_CALLBACK_ENTRY and guarded callback
+completion separately bind the exact same Buffer hash. None alone proves relay
+kernel receipt. RAW_BACKEND_CALLBACK_ENTRY is entry into the JS callback, not a
+kernel-arrival timestamp. Guard entry→exit includes spawn/wait/instrumentation
+wall latency, not isolated Python CPU time. All overhead is visible and prevents
+an unsupported causal comparison with the original uninstrumented failure.
+
+Each exchange outcome is exclusively persisted on resolve OR reject, retaining
+elapsed duration, original partial ACK/received/EOSE/sent-frame state and first
+error. Failed socket cleanup follows terminal evidence; evidence-write errors
+remain failures. The baseline requires a positive publication ACK plus exactly
+one queried original event and its actual EOSE. Query responses remain semantic
+whole-event comparisons without imposing JSON object key order. No EVENT/EOSE,
+callback, backend receipt or success result is synthesized by runtime code.
+
+Raw diagnostic observer phases can narrow callback/guard/timer uncertainty.
+They cannot by themselves prove internal relay DB queue/checkpoint/load shedding,
+CPU scheduling cause, memory exhaustion or Amethyst incompatibility. Operational
+relay logging is retained if actually available; this profile does not invent
+logs or silently change RUST_LOG/configuration. A no-Android baseline success is
+not proof that the original app traffic was causal.
+
